\caption{Cross-model count curves for Gemini 2.5 Flash (API) and Qwen3-32B on FewRel RE (classification accuracy). 25 batches per count level, same samples and batch compositions (seed 42). Gemini's count-1 result is included for completeness; Qwen3 count-1 was not collected. Both models show monotonic decline from count 4 onward ($\rho = -1.0$ for both on counts 4--32). $^\dagger$Count 64: Gemini based on 11/25 batches (partial).}
\label{tab:cross_model_gemini}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_gemini} confirms that the count-related accuracy decline replicates on Gemini 2.5 Flash. From count 4 to count 32, both models show perfectly monotonic decline (Spearman $\rho = -1.0$). Including count 1, the Gemini count curve yields $\rho = -0.900$ ($p = 0.037$); including partial count-64 data (11/25 batches), $\rho = -0.943$ ($p = 0.005$). The decline magnitude is smaller for Gemini ($-$13.3\,pp from count 4 to 32) than for Qwen3 ($-$21.8\,pp), consistent with Gemini's higher absolute accuracy providing more headroom before degradation saturates. At each count level, Gemini matches or exceeds Qwen3, with the gap widening at higher counts (0.3\,pp at count 4 vs.\ 8.7\,pp at count 32, 9.8\,pp at partial count 64); Gemini appears more robust to schema scaling but not immune. Dimension~2 (BCS-controlled confusability) has not been tested on proprietary models; cross-architecture Dimension~2 validation remains for future work.

\section{Few-Shot Prompting and Confusability}
\label{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}

To test whether in-context demonstrations mitigate the BCS-associated confusability effect, we evaluate 0-shot, 1-shot, and 5-shot prompting on FewRel 80-relation RE at count 8 using the same extreme-BCS batch design as \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}. Each condition uses 50 batches (25 low-BCS, mean $\approx 0.36$; 25 high-BCS, mean $\approx 0.80$), drawn from the pre-constructed batch pool of \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}. Few-shot examples are sampled from the FewRel training split for each target type.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Low-BCS} & \textbf{High-BCS} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (pp)} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
0-shot & 0.682 & 0.498 & +18.5 & {[}+14.3, +22.7{]} \\
1-shot & 0.754 & 0.554 & +20.0 & {[}+16.3, +23.5{]} \\
5-shot & 0.715 & 0.488 & +22.8 & {[}+18.9, +26.8{]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{BCS gap under few-shot prompting (FewRel, count 8). Demonstrations improve absolute accuracy on low-BCS batches (+3.3--7.2\,pp over 0-shot) but provide inconsistent benefit on high-BCS batches (+5.6\,pp at 1-shot, $-$1.0\,pp at 5-shot). The confusability gap widens from +18.5\,pp (0-shot) to +22.8\,pp (5-shot). All metrics are micro-averaged across batches.}
\label{tab:fewshot_bcs}
\end{table}

Demonstrations improve absolute accuracy primarily on low-BCS (easy) batches, where type boundaries are already distinct and examples reinforce correct mappings. On high-BCS batches, where types are semantically confusable, demonstrations provide inconsistent benefit (+5.6\,pp at 1-shot, $-$1.0\,pp at 5-shot): the model still substitutes similar types at comparable rates. The net effect is that the confusability gap widens under few-shot prompting (+4.3\,pp from 0-shot to 5-shot, bootstrap $p = 0.014$), with all three confidence intervals excluding zero. This indicates that BCS-associated type confusion is a structural property of the schema composition rather than a deficit addressable through prompt engineering. The non-monotonic low-BCS pattern (0.754 at 1-shot vs.\ 0.715 at 5-shot) may reflect lost-in-middle effects from increased prompt length \cite{lost-in-middle-2024}.

The 0-shot gap (+18.5\,pp) is smaller than the extreme-batch gap reported in \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled} (+25.5\,pp) because the two experiments use different test splits and batch sampling seeds; direct magnitude comparison across experiments is not appropriate.

